  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "have: affirmative with symptoms (I have a headache)",
      "title_form": "Have para síntomas: I have a headache",
      "title_target": "Have for symptoms: I have a headache",
      "description_md_form": "Para decir que tienes un síntoma, usa **have** (tener) + el síntoma. Es una forma fija y muy común en inglés.\n\n- **I have** a headache. = Tengo dolor de cabeza.\n- **I have** a cold. = Tengo frío (resfriado).\n\n**Importante:** en inglés no se dice *I am headache*. Usa **have**, no **be**. La estructura es simple:\n\n**I have + síntoma**\n\nCon otras personas: **you have**, **he/she has**. Para A1, practica con **I have** y **you have**.",
      "description_md_target": "To say you have a symptom, use **have** + the symptom. It is a fixed and very common form in English.\n\n- **I have** a headache. = I have a headache.\n- **I have** a cold. = I have a cold.\n\n**Important:** in English we do not say *I am headache*. Use **have**, not **be**. The structure is simple:\n\n**I have + symptom**\n\nWith other people: **you have**, **he/she has**. For A1, practice with **I have** and **you have**.",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + have + symptom",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "present_simple_have",
        "headers": [
          "Pronombre",
          "Forma",
          "Ejemplo"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "I",
